WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.119 --> 00:00:02.248
Soldier: Pull out, Graham! Pull!

00:00:02.248 --> 00:00:03.081
Pull!

00:00:07.147 --> 00:00:08.640
[splashes]

00:00:08.640 --> 00:00:10.860
Remi Adeleke: Yeah, it's
not that easy. [laughs]

00:00:10.860 --> 00:00:12.420
What's up? My name is Remi Adeleke.

00:00:12.420 --> 00:00:14.360
I spent 13 years in the military

00:00:14.360 --> 00:00:15.390
with the majority of that time

00:00:15.390 --> 00:00:17.760
spent in special
operations as a Navy SEAL.

00:00:17.760 --> 00:00:19.740
And today I'm gonna be breaking down

00:00:19.740 --> 00:00:23.040
some iconic Navy SEAL movies and TV shows.

00:00:26.149 --> 00:00:27.760
[helicopter roaring]

00:00:27.760 --> 00:00:29.179
Soldier: Everyone good to go?

00:00:29.179 --> 00:00:30.370
All: Good to go.

00:00:33.590 --> 00:00:34.615
Remi: Yeah, I've never seen

00:00:34.615 --> 00:00:36.765
any devices like that. [laughs]

00:00:42.210 --> 00:00:44.301
We're called SEALs.

00:00:44.301 --> 00:00:46.601
SEAL is actually a
acronym, sea, air, land.

00:00:46.601 --> 00:00:47.900
A lot of people don't know that.

00:00:47.900 --> 00:00:51.550
So it's typical for us
to work in the water.

00:00:51.550 --> 00:00:53.890
I've jumped out of a plane
into the water before.

00:00:53.890 --> 00:00:55.860
I've jumped out of a helicopter
into the water before.

00:00:55.860 --> 00:00:57.635
In the scene, they're jumping,

00:00:57.635 --> 00:00:58.751
not only are they jumping out,

00:00:58.751 --> 00:01:00.040
but they're pushing vehicles,

00:01:00.040 --> 00:01:03.290
underwater vehicles into the water.

00:01:03.290 --> 00:01:04.840
Now, those type of vehicles,

00:01:04.840 --> 00:01:07.215
I've never seen in my life. [laughs]

00:01:08.640 --> 00:01:12.294
There are vehicles that
we do use called SDVs.

00:01:12.294 --> 00:01:14.445
It stands for SEAL delivery vehicles.

00:01:14.445 --> 00:01:16.550
Goodspeed: Move in! Good to go.

00:01:16.550 --> 00:01:17.950
Remi: There are times when,

00:01:17.950 --> 00:01:22.110
if somebody has a unique specialization

00:01:22.110 --> 00:01:23.370
but they're not a SEAL,

00:01:23.370 --> 00:01:26.032
they will go on a mission,

00:01:26.032 --> 00:01:28.710
and they will be embedded
within the SEAL platoon.

00:01:28.710 --> 00:01:31.160
However, they're not gonna be

00:01:31.160 --> 00:01:31.993
at the front of the train.

00:01:31.993 --> 00:01:33.840
They're not gonna be the first
one in the door. [laughs]

00:01:33.840 --> 00:01:34.980
That's for sure.

00:01:34.980 --> 00:01:37.430
And they're gonna be
somewhere in the middle,

00:01:37.430 --> 00:01:39.846
somewhere protected and
somewhere where they can't

00:01:39.846 --> 00:01:42.825
hurt the other guys within the platoon.

00:01:42.825 --> 00:01:44.440
"The Rock" inspired me.

00:01:44.440 --> 00:01:46.570
That was the first time I
was exposed to Navy SEALs.

00:01:46.570 --> 00:01:48.760
I had never heard of Navy SEALs before,

00:01:48.760 --> 00:01:50.730
and I went to go watch the film.

00:01:50.730 --> 00:01:52.360
And when I saw these guys,

00:01:52.360 --> 00:01:53.480
that really intrigued me.

00:01:53.480 --> 00:01:55.710
And then when we got to the scene where

00:01:55.710 --> 00:01:59.400
they actually sacrifice
themselves to save other people,

00:01:59.400 --> 00:02:01.248
that's what really kind
of stuck out to me,

00:02:01.248 --> 00:02:05.960
this idea that these
men are so close-knit,

00:02:05.960 --> 00:02:07.850
they're skilled,

00:02:07.850 --> 00:02:09.660
they're great at what they do,

00:02:09.660 --> 00:02:11.635
and they're willing to put their lives

00:02:11.635 --> 00:02:13.620
on the line for other people.

00:02:13.620 --> 00:02:15.630
I've worked with Michael
Bay on some other films,

00:02:15.630 --> 00:02:17.600
so gotta be careful.

00:02:17.600 --> 00:02:22.125
I would say I would give it
a 8.5 as far as accuracy.

00:02:22.125 --> 00:02:23.508
[yelling]

00:02:26.200 --> 00:02:28.210
It's supposed to be boats
on heads, but they have,

00:02:28.210 --> 00:02:29.350
they're holding it with their hands,

00:02:29.350 --> 00:02:30.600
and we've never done that.

00:02:30.600 --> 00:02:33.270
There's an instructor in the boat.

00:02:33.270 --> 00:02:34.510
Nah, that's not gonna happen.

00:02:34.510 --> 00:02:36.100
The boat is heavy as it is.

00:02:36.100 --> 00:02:36.933
Instructor: Move it! Move it!

00:02:36.933 --> 00:02:38.680
Remi: The bell, yep.

00:02:38.680 --> 00:02:40.330
The bell. That's definitely real.

00:02:42.070 --> 00:02:44.320
I know a lot of guys
who have rang that bell.

00:02:45.220 --> 00:02:48.563
Now, these things, I'm
assuming that these are buoys.

00:02:50.410 --> 00:02:52.530
We never did that at all.

00:02:52.530 --> 00:02:54.870
We never pushed buoys through a,

00:02:54.870 --> 00:02:57.330
looks like a berm obstacle course,

00:02:57.330 --> 00:02:58.550
so I don't know what that is.

00:02:58.550 --> 00:03:00.850
And here we have the
buoy rolling over people.

00:03:01.875 --> 00:03:03.440
That's not happening.

00:03:03.440 --> 00:03:05.140
I mean, that's more of a liability.

00:03:08.910 --> 00:03:11.430
Now, this type of surf torture,

00:03:11.430 --> 00:03:12.940
I have never seen it.

00:03:12.940 --> 00:03:14.990
I don't know what kind of push-up that is.

00:03:16.049 --> 00:03:17.970
[wave crashes]

00:03:17.970 --> 00:03:19.360
It's a mental exercise.

00:03:19.360 --> 00:03:20.360
At least for me it was.

00:03:20.360 --> 00:03:23.330
You're laying in this freezing cold water,

00:03:23.330 --> 00:03:25.260
you don't know how long
you're gonna be in the water,

00:03:25.260 --> 00:03:27.150
and that's where they get
a lot of guys who quit.

00:03:27.150 --> 00:03:29.150
That's where you do get a lot of quitters.

00:03:29.150 --> 00:03:32.250
Me, there were multiple
times during hell week

00:03:32.250 --> 00:03:34.900
and various evolutions
where, during surf torture,

00:03:34.900 --> 00:03:37.897
I was either near
hypothermia or hypothermic.

00:03:37.897 --> 00:03:39.889
As a matter of fact, during
one surf torture in hell week

00:03:39.889 --> 00:03:42.420
my core temperature dropped
down to 88.7 degrees.

00:03:42.420 --> 00:03:45.120
So. [laughs] And what happened after that

00:03:45.120 --> 00:03:47.976
was the instructors took
me to BUD/S medical,

00:03:47.976 --> 00:03:49.860
went through a rewarming drill.

00:03:49.860 --> 00:03:51.980
I think it took about a
hour or two to rewarm me.

00:03:51.980 --> 00:03:55.610
And then as soon as I was back
to a normal core temperature,

00:03:55.610 --> 00:03:57.340
I went right back into hell week,

00:03:57.340 --> 00:03:59.020
and when the instructors
asked me, they said,

00:03:59.020 --> 00:04:00.360
"Hey, you wanna quit
or do you wanna go back

00:04:00.360 --> 00:04:01.193
in that cold water?"

00:04:01.193 --> 00:04:03.895
And I said, "I'll go back
in the cold water." So.

00:04:03.895 --> 00:04:06.236
[yelling]

00:04:07.660 --> 00:04:09.670
The instructors are trying
to put you in a situation

00:04:09.670 --> 00:04:12.330
where you have to work together as a team

00:04:12.330 --> 00:04:14.670
and push through the discomfort.

00:04:14.670 --> 00:04:17.230
There's cases where, if
you're not running in sync,

00:04:17.230 --> 00:04:20.830
the boat will bob and
bounce on a person's head

00:04:20.830 --> 00:04:22.480
and that just causes more pain.

00:04:22.480 --> 00:04:24.140
There's actually been guys

00:04:24.140 --> 00:04:25.980
who have had serious neck injuries

00:04:25.980 --> 00:04:29.300
and fractured their necks
because of the bouncing

00:04:29.300 --> 00:04:31.962
or because somebody within
the boat was ducking boat,

00:04:31.962 --> 00:04:35.503
which means they had their head down

00:04:35.503 --> 00:04:37.010
while other people were
carrying the weight.

00:04:37.010 --> 00:04:39.730
So it's a pretty serious evolution.

00:04:39.730 --> 00:04:41.250
And the instructor in the boat, eh,

00:04:41.250 --> 00:04:43.861
I would have to give it about a four.

00:04:47.690 --> 00:04:49.193
Instructor: Six times three.

00:04:51.644 --> 00:04:53.319
Hurry up, hurry up, hurry up.

00:04:53.319 --> 00:04:55.482
Remi: What we're seeing here is a swim,

00:04:55.482 --> 00:04:58.060
it's called wetsuit appreciation swim.

00:04:58.060 --> 00:05:01.450
And essentially they make you
swim in freezing cold water

00:05:01.450 --> 00:05:03.020
for a long period of
time without a wetsuit.

00:05:03.020 --> 00:05:05.880
And it's miserable.

00:05:05.880 --> 00:05:07.021
It's horrible.

00:05:10.231 --> 00:05:11.797
Instructor: Andrew! Come back!

00:05:11.797 --> 00:05:15.100
Remi: Yeah, this guy,
he's a shallow water,

00:05:15.100 --> 00:05:16.060
shallow water blackout.

00:05:16.060 --> 00:05:17.908
You know, he blacked out
from being on the water.

00:05:17.908 --> 00:05:19.825
The majority of it is footage from

00:05:19.825 --> 00:05:23.540
a Discovery Channel
documentary called "BUD/S 234."

00:05:23.540 --> 00:05:26.720
And, interestingly, I
watched that documentary

00:05:26.720 --> 00:05:28.980
in order to prepare for
BUD/S, 'cause, you know,

00:05:28.980 --> 00:05:33.020
coming from the Bronx, and I
didn't have a lot of exposure

00:05:33.020 --> 00:05:36.080
to Navy SEALs or how to
prepare for SEAL training.

00:05:36.080 --> 00:05:37.330
As a matter of fact,
when I joined the Navy,

00:05:37.330 --> 00:05:38.238
I couldn't swim.

00:05:41.974 --> 00:05:42.807
Instructor: Two minutes!

00:05:42.807 --> 00:05:44.911
You don't make it, you do it again!

00:05:44.911 --> 00:05:45.927
Instructor: Have you been tested this hard

00:05:45.927 --> 00:05:46.769
ever in your life?

00:05:46.769 --> 00:05:47.602
Trainee: Never.

00:05:47.602 --> 00:05:50.610
Remi: BUD/S is six months
broken up into three phases.

00:05:50.610 --> 00:05:52.575
First phase, which is where they really

00:05:52.575 --> 00:05:54.330
weed out the majority of the class.

00:05:54.330 --> 00:05:57.290
Then there's second phase,
which is dive phase.

00:05:57.290 --> 00:05:59.770
That's when they're
beginning to really teach you

00:05:59.770 --> 00:06:01.600
different elements of being a SEAL.

00:06:01.600 --> 00:06:04.210
And then third phase is, weapons, tactics,

00:06:04.210 --> 00:06:05.670
land warfare, and demolitions.

00:06:05.670 --> 00:06:07.530
If a person makes it
through six months of BUD/S,

00:06:07.530 --> 00:06:10.270
then next step is SQT, SEAL
qualification training.

00:06:10.270 --> 00:06:12.240
That's where you really get into the weeds

00:06:12.240 --> 00:06:14.540
of what it means to be a Navy SEAL

00:06:14.540 --> 00:06:16.130
and to do the job that we do.

00:06:16.130 --> 00:06:18.237
And then from there you
go on to your SEAL team.

00:06:18.237 --> 00:06:20.060
For an attrition rate,

00:06:20.060 --> 00:06:23.650
for SEAL training, fluctuates
between 75 and 90%.

00:06:23.650 --> 00:06:26.980
The class that I graduated
with started out with 270,

00:06:26.980 --> 00:06:29.680
and we only graduated 29 guys.

00:06:29.680 --> 00:06:31.690
I have to give it a 10.

00:06:31.690 --> 00:06:33.010
I have to give it 'cause
it's the real deal.

00:06:33.010 --> 00:06:34.710
You don't get no realer
than that. [laughs]

00:06:35.666 --> 00:06:36.582
Jack: Listen to me. Listen.

00:06:36.582 --> 00:06:37.415
Hey, listen up.

00:06:37.415 --> 00:06:39.530
When you go outside
that gate, you go left.

00:06:39.530 --> 00:06:40.705
You understand?
Rone: Yes.

00:06:40.705 --> 00:06:42.729
Jack: The right is bad
guys. You are going left.

00:06:42.729 --> 00:06:43.920
You get in the back way to the annex.

00:06:43.920 --> 00:06:45.102
You got it?
Rone: I got it.

00:06:45.102 --> 00:06:47.435
Remi: This guy who's driving

00:06:47.435 --> 00:06:49.327
and he seems disoriented

00:06:49.327 --> 00:06:50.446
and he's not paying attention to this,

00:06:50.446 --> 00:06:51.960
'cause most likely he's not a SEAL.

00:06:51.960 --> 00:06:54.749
If he was a SEAL, he
wouldn't be as confused.

00:06:54.749 --> 00:06:57.486
[gunshots]
[shouting]

00:06:57.486 --> 00:06:58.990
Jack: Peel it! Peel it! Peel it!

00:06:58.990 --> 00:07:00.930
Someone cover us on the roof!

00:07:00.930 --> 00:07:02.708
Remi: What was going on
was the guys were bounding.

00:07:02.708 --> 00:07:06.060
And when a unit is bounding
away from a contact,

00:07:06.060 --> 00:07:09.700
it's always important
for one or two or three,

00:07:09.700 --> 00:07:11.530
however many guys, to be shooting

00:07:11.530 --> 00:07:14.550
and the other part of that
team moving, you know,

00:07:14.550 --> 00:07:16.840
we call that shoot, move or cover move.

00:07:16.840 --> 00:07:18.580
So, in the clip, you know,

00:07:18.580 --> 00:07:20.820
John's character said, "cover and move,"

00:07:20.820 --> 00:07:22.410
and that's something we always do

00:07:22.410 --> 00:07:23.950
when we're bounding from a contact.

00:07:23.950 --> 00:07:27.610
Those are all weapons
that not only, you know,

00:07:27.610 --> 00:07:30.360
I've carried, but I've seen
other guys in my platoon,

00:07:30.360 --> 00:07:32.900
in my teams carry when we go
out the door on operations.

00:07:32.900 --> 00:07:35.530
This movie, "13 Hours," is based off of

00:07:35.530 --> 00:07:37.970
an actual event that happened in Benghazi.

00:07:37.970 --> 00:07:41.100
The embassy was attacked and overrun.

00:07:41.100 --> 00:07:43.410
The guys who were defending,
for the most part,

00:07:43.410 --> 00:07:46.373
they were all guys who have
special operations backgrounds.

00:07:47.230 --> 00:07:48.290
A few of them were Navy SEALs.

00:07:48.290 --> 00:07:51.120
Actually, one of them, the
guy who got killed, Ty.

00:07:51.120 --> 00:07:53.210
Ty Woods was my BUD/S instructor.

00:07:53.210 --> 00:07:54.640
He put me through SEAL training.

00:07:54.640 --> 00:07:56.847
Personally, I would give it a 9.5.

00:07:56.847 --> 00:07:59.930
[helicopter roaring]

00:08:01.170 --> 00:08:03.700
So, they're fast-roping.
I've fast-roped before.

00:08:03.700 --> 00:08:08.700
Fast rope, it's a easy way
to insert into a situation.

00:08:08.880 --> 00:08:10.333
Pilot: Continue, you're clear.

00:08:14.240 --> 00:08:16.300
Remi: This guy right here,
he's an intelligence guy.

00:08:16.300 --> 00:08:18.650
He's a SEAL, but he's
also an intelligence guy.

00:08:19.800 --> 00:08:22.180
We call it HUMINT, which
is human intelligence,

00:08:22.180 --> 00:08:23.923
and that's what I did in the SEAL teams.

00:08:26.720 --> 00:08:28.220
Not only are they covering down on doors

00:08:28.220 --> 00:08:30.630
as one guy's, as the point
man is moving forward,

00:08:30.630 --> 00:08:32.120
but they're covering down on windows.

00:08:32.120 --> 00:08:34.390
So, everything is a threat
when you're on operation,

00:08:34.390 --> 00:08:37.460
especially, you know, on a ship like this,

00:08:37.460 --> 00:08:40.690
or a house, you know, closets,

00:08:40.690 --> 00:08:42.760
open doors,

00:08:42.760 --> 00:08:44.240
windows,

00:08:44.240 --> 00:08:45.737
hidden spaces.

00:08:45.737 --> 00:08:47.900
Soldier: Clear. Bridge secure.

00:08:48.770 --> 00:08:50.510
Remi: When one guy says clear,

00:08:50.510 --> 00:08:53.190
clear is just, that's something we say

00:08:53.190 --> 00:08:54.270
when the room is clear.

00:08:54.270 --> 00:08:55.710
That doesn't mean that
nobody's in the room.

00:08:55.710 --> 00:08:58.083
That means that the room is clear.

00:08:59.330 --> 00:09:02.870
[boat engine roaring]

00:09:02.870 --> 00:09:05.510
The SOC boats that you saw in the clip,

00:09:05.510 --> 00:09:07.380
they're really high speed.

00:09:07.380 --> 00:09:10.991
They're operated by a special
operations unit called SWCC.

00:09:10.991 --> 00:09:14.430
SWCC stands for special
warfare combat-craft crewman.

00:09:14.430 --> 00:09:16.810
On the rivers and any
operation along the water,

00:09:16.810 --> 00:09:20.250
they help insert SEALs and
extract SEALs from targets.

00:09:20.250 --> 00:09:23.191
They used real Navy SEALs

00:09:23.191 --> 00:09:25.500
and real SWCC operators

00:09:25.500 --> 00:09:26.991
as the main actors,

00:09:26.991 --> 00:09:28.093
so I would give it a 9.5.

00:09:28.093 --> 00:09:30.980
The 0.5 that's missing is
the fact that, you know,

00:09:30.980 --> 00:09:33.747
in Hollywood, you gotta

00:09:33.747 --> 00:09:35.745
[laughs] you gotta
spice it up a little bit

00:09:35.745 --> 00:09:38.137
to have a storyline. I
get that as a writer, so.

00:09:40.520 --> 00:09:42.700
Soldier: Jesus, Dane,
who packed your chute?

00:09:42.700 --> 00:09:44.480
It doesn't look good.

00:09:44.480 --> 00:09:46.350
Remi: We always check each other's gear.

00:09:46.350 --> 00:09:49.603
My platoon chief had a saying:
"Check, check, and recheck."

00:09:49.603 --> 00:09:52.247
Yeah, this is a HALO.
Standard HALO. Jumping out.

00:09:55.341 --> 00:09:56.380
Soldier: Oh, shit!

00:09:56.380 --> 00:09:57.355
Remi: So, what's going on here,

00:09:57.355 --> 00:09:58.988
this guy has a malfunction.

00:10:03.420 --> 00:10:05.845
Yeah, he's,

00:10:05.845 --> 00:10:10.128
this reserve opens pretty late,

00:10:10.128 --> 00:10:11.770
probably 'cause he cut away late.

00:10:15.990 --> 00:10:18.830
Yeah, it's not that easy. [laughs]

00:10:18.830 --> 00:10:21.960
If you have a malfunction
and you've tried to fix it

00:10:21.960 --> 00:10:26.210
and it didn't work, you gotta cut away.

00:10:26.210 --> 00:10:27.730
And essentially what it is,

00:10:27.730 --> 00:10:30.060
you look down, and you
look down at that red tab,

00:10:30.060 --> 00:10:32.730
and you pull that cutaway first,

00:10:32.730 --> 00:10:34.530
and then you look down at your reserve,

00:10:34.530 --> 00:10:36.730
and you pull that away
second, in that order.

00:10:36.730 --> 00:10:38.280
There have been people who've died,

00:10:38.280 --> 00:10:41.340
not just in the military
but in civilian jumping

00:10:41.340 --> 00:10:42.860
because they had a malfunction

00:10:42.860 --> 00:10:44.570
and they made it worse by opening

00:10:44.570 --> 00:10:47.060
their reserve parachute
before cutting away,

00:10:47.060 --> 00:10:49.320
and their reserve
parachute now gets tangled

00:10:49.320 --> 00:10:52.190
in their main parachute, and, I mean,

00:10:52.190 --> 00:10:53.630
it's nothing you can do after that.

00:10:53.630 --> 00:10:54.463
I have to give it a,

00:10:54.463 --> 00:10:56.450
I'd have to give it a five, man. [laughs]

00:10:56.450 --> 00:10:57.700
I have to give it a five.

00:11:00.851 --> 00:11:03.660
[gunshots]

00:11:03.660 --> 00:11:05.746
Soldier: Echo 02, moving to deck three.

00:11:06.756 --> 00:11:07.590
[gunshot]

00:11:07.590 --> 00:11:09.120
Remi: First and foremost,
they're operating at night.

00:11:09.120 --> 00:11:13.520
Every, this is a DA. It's a direct action.

00:11:13.520 --> 00:11:15.090
They're going after a specific target.

00:11:15.090 --> 00:11:16.270
I've done a lot of these,

00:11:16.270 --> 00:11:20.350
and 99% of them have been at night.

00:11:20.350 --> 00:11:22.090
I think I heard three shots initially,

00:11:22.090 --> 00:11:23.518
and then he shot him again
while he was on the ground.

00:11:23.518 --> 00:11:26.130
People say, well, that's excessive.

00:11:26.130 --> 00:11:28.220
We do that for a reason. 'Cause, you know,

00:11:28.220 --> 00:11:29.560
it's not like in the movies.

00:11:29.560 --> 00:11:31.060
In the movies, you shoot
a guy one time, he falls.

00:11:31.060 --> 00:11:32.560
In reality, you know, you can shoot a guy

00:11:32.560 --> 00:11:34.910
six, seven, eight times,
and he can still fight, so.

00:11:37.044 --> 00:11:39.850
See, they're really silent.
It's just hand squeezes,

00:11:39.850 --> 00:11:42.510
and there's no verbal communication.

00:11:46.010 --> 00:11:48.322
I mean, it was a mirror image of

00:11:48.322 --> 00:11:49.846
a lot of operations I've been on,

00:11:49.846 --> 00:11:50.913
as far as, like, the movements

00:11:50.913 --> 00:11:53.621
and the tactics and sneaking through.

00:11:55.680 --> 00:11:58.418
Those lasers that you
see, we can see them,

00:11:58.418 --> 00:12:00.200
because of the type of
camera that was used.

00:12:00.200 --> 00:12:03.310
But we use IR lasers on our weapons.

00:12:03.310 --> 00:12:04.380
Typically, in some movies,

00:12:04.380 --> 00:12:06.880
you'll see the red lasers
or green lasers on guns.

00:12:06.880 --> 00:12:08.360
That's super unrealistic.

00:12:08.360 --> 00:12:10.710
We use hand signals or, you know,

00:12:10.710 --> 00:12:12.160
or we use our weapons

00:12:12.160 --> 00:12:14.410
as a way to communicate with each other

00:12:14.410 --> 00:12:18.030
so that we can stay stealth
and stay completely silent

00:12:18.030 --> 00:12:20.635
and not tip off the enemy.

00:12:20.635 --> 00:12:22.134
Soldier: Clear.

00:12:22.134 --> 00:12:24.176
[woman grunts]

00:12:25.976 --> 00:12:27.077
No vests.

00:12:27.077 --> 00:12:28.140
All clear.

00:12:28.140 --> 00:12:29.155
Remi: When they came across the women,

00:12:29.155 --> 00:12:31.113
the first thing that they did

00:12:31.113 --> 00:12:32.921
was they searched them,

00:12:32.921 --> 00:12:34.900
and they were searching
them for a suicide vest.

00:12:34.900 --> 00:12:39.600
The enemy will put suicide
vests on women and children.

00:12:39.600 --> 00:12:41.050
I remember when there was
a guy we were tracking

00:12:41.050 --> 00:12:42.456
for a long period of time,

00:12:42.456 --> 00:12:43.630
and I won't mention
the country we were in,

00:12:43.630 --> 00:12:45.330
where he would recruit, you know,

00:12:45.330 --> 00:12:49.580
kids 9, 8, 7, 11, 12, 13,

00:12:49.580 --> 00:12:52.020
and put suicide vests on them.

00:12:52.020 --> 00:12:55.010
Rating for this one, this
scene, this specific scene,

00:12:55.010 --> 00:12:58.120
because of the way they moved tactically,

00:12:58.120 --> 00:12:59.304
I would give it a 9.5.

00:12:59.304 --> 00:13:01.270
Waters: I'd just like to

00:13:02.980 --> 00:13:04.713
hear what you guys have to say about it.

00:13:04.713 --> 00:13:06.046
That's all. Speak freely.

00:13:08.200 --> 00:13:10.375
Lake: My opinion, sir,

00:13:10.375 --> 00:13:12.041
we cut our losses.

00:13:12.041 --> 00:13:13.650
This isn't our war.

00:13:13.650 --> 00:13:15.350
Remi: That is one of my
favorite Navy SEAL movies.

00:13:15.350 --> 00:13:16.590
I'll say that.

00:13:16.590 --> 00:13:17.573
I'll say that first and foremost.

00:13:17.573 --> 00:13:19.089
It was directed by a phenomenal director

00:13:19.089 --> 00:13:20.470
by the name of Antoine Fuqua.

00:13:20.470 --> 00:13:25.030
It really shows the
interaction between SEALs,

00:13:25.030 --> 00:13:27.410
but not just, not just
SEALs, but, you know,

00:13:27.410 --> 00:13:28.996
the leadership of a SEAL platoon

00:13:28.996 --> 00:13:32.870
and the other guys, the
sled dogs, so to speak.

00:13:32.870 --> 00:13:35.450
We have a saying, again,
"Leadership at every level."

00:13:35.450 --> 00:13:37.790
And so we all are able to give our input.

00:13:37.790 --> 00:13:41.282
I've been in situations with my OIC,

00:13:41.282 --> 00:13:43.350
OIC's officer in charge of the platoon,

00:13:43.350 --> 00:13:46.170
and where he wants everybody's input,

00:13:46.170 --> 00:13:48.770
he wants to hear, "Hey,
how should we take down,

00:13:48.770 --> 00:13:50.398
how should we take down this
target? What should we do?"

00:13:50.398 --> 00:13:51.850
And it's not like other military units

00:13:51.850 --> 00:13:53.730
where you just had, you
got one guy in charge,

00:13:53.730 --> 00:13:55.970
and, you know, he says,
"Go storm that mountain,"

00:13:55.970 --> 00:13:58.580
and everybody just mindlessly
goes and storms that mountain.

00:13:58.580 --> 00:14:00.090
Interestingly, a lot of
people don't know this,

00:14:00.090 --> 00:14:01.762
but the requirements academically

00:14:01.762 --> 00:14:04.320
to get in SEAL training
are really, really high.

00:14:04.320 --> 00:14:06.011
And a lot of guys who do show up

00:14:06.011 --> 00:14:07.794
to the SEAL training, BUD/S,

00:14:07.794 --> 00:14:09.210
they have bachelor's degrees.

00:14:09.210 --> 00:14:10.790
A lot of them have master's degrees.

00:14:10.790 --> 00:14:12.680
There were guys who were established.

00:14:12.680 --> 00:14:14.360
I mean, there were guys
who were accountants

00:14:14.360 --> 00:14:18.150
and lawyers or big businessmen,

00:14:18.150 --> 00:14:19.630
and one day they woke up and said,

00:14:19.630 --> 00:14:20.510
"You know what? This is boring.

00:14:20.510 --> 00:14:22.970
I wanna be a Navy SEAL." [laughs]

00:14:22.970 --> 00:14:26.910
Yeah. You know, that scene,
I would give it a 9.5.

00:14:26.910 --> 00:14:28.300
It's hard for me to give anything a 10,

00:14:28.300 --> 00:14:31.194
but, yeah, for that
scene, absolutely a 9.5.

00:14:31.194 --> 00:14:33.444
[gunshots]

00:14:37.602 --> 00:14:39.769
[screams]

00:14:41.303 --> 00:14:44.053
[explosion]

00:14:45.660 --> 00:14:47.510
Not only these guys covering and moving,

00:14:47.510 --> 00:14:50.930
but they had proper 360 security,

00:14:50.930 --> 00:14:54.180
which allowed that one SEAL
to see that suicide bomber.

00:14:54.180 --> 00:14:55.570
As soon as they took contact,

00:14:55.570 --> 00:14:57.510
they immediately started firing back.

00:14:57.510 --> 00:14:59.760
And that's something that we always do.

00:14:59.760 --> 00:15:01.490
We, you know, suppress the threat.

00:15:01.490 --> 00:15:03.860
And then, yeah, again,
we have 360 security.

00:15:03.860 --> 00:15:06.680
So, the contact was in the front,

00:15:06.680 --> 00:15:09.430
and, you know, again, SEALs
are not mindless robots.

00:15:09.430 --> 00:15:10.990
So everybody didn't just start shooting

00:15:10.990 --> 00:15:12.170
at the contact in the front.

00:15:12.170 --> 00:15:14.690
If somebody gets injured
in the field, you know,

00:15:14.690 --> 00:15:17.610
first and foremost, if they
can take care of themselves,

00:15:17.610 --> 00:15:20.125
they take care of themselves
in the middle of the battle.

00:15:20.125 --> 00:15:22.280
And then once somebody can get to them,

00:15:22.280 --> 00:15:23.660
then that person, you know,

00:15:23.660 --> 00:15:25.410
goes and gets them and tends to them.

00:15:25.410 --> 00:15:26.830
In the Navy, we don't call medics, medics.

00:15:26.830 --> 00:15:27.721
We call them corpsmen.

00:15:27.721 --> 00:15:28.820
And once that corpsman can get to them

00:15:28.820 --> 00:15:32.260
after the threat has been
suppressed at that point,

00:15:32.260 --> 00:15:34.220
then they'll go tend to them.

00:15:34.220 --> 00:15:36.310
Eric: Bravo 6, say again your last.

00:15:36.310 --> 00:15:38.890
Ray: 6 to base, I say again. Fallen eagle.

00:15:38.890 --> 00:15:40.310
Remi: When somebody gets
killed on operation,

00:15:40.310 --> 00:15:42.260
you know, you win the fight,

00:15:42.260 --> 00:15:46.829
and then after you win
the fight, you know,

00:15:46.829 --> 00:15:49.020
you help, you know, transport,

00:15:49.020 --> 00:15:50.270
transport them back to base

00:15:50.270 --> 00:15:51.800
and give them a proper ceremony.

00:15:51.800 --> 00:15:53.433
Soldier: We lost Bravo 1.

00:15:54.620 --> 00:15:56.521
Eric: How long till we
can get him out of there?

00:15:57.941 --> 00:16:01.210
Remi: I would give that
scene specifically a 9.5.

00:16:01.210 --> 00:16:02.950
Commander: I need sights on targets.

00:16:02.950 --> 00:16:04.803
I need three targets green.

00:16:09.430 --> 00:16:11.720
Remi: Passing comms to the snipers

00:16:11.720 --> 00:16:12.871
who are gonna take the target

00:16:12.871 --> 00:16:15.840
so they're aware of what's going on.

00:16:15.840 --> 00:16:17.190
Again, going back to communication.

00:16:17.190 --> 00:16:18.498
Communication's key.

00:16:18.498 --> 00:16:19.513
Commander: Stop the tow.

00:16:23.320 --> 00:16:24.349
Execute.

00:16:24.349 --> 00:16:26.770
[gunshots]

00:16:26.770 --> 00:16:27.791
Phillips: What was that?

00:16:27.791 --> 00:16:28.624
Remi: He could have been a commander.

00:16:28.624 --> 00:16:30.890
Commander's actually a rank,

00:16:30.890 --> 00:16:33.100
but for the sake of just
explaining this point,

00:16:33.100 --> 00:16:35.200
you know, we had a commander

00:16:35.200 --> 00:16:37.090
who was definitely a Navy SEAL,

00:16:37.090 --> 00:16:39.915
and he's giving commands,

00:16:39.915 --> 00:16:41.348
but he's also giving the guys,

00:16:41.348 --> 00:16:43.290
you know, giving the guys
freedom to do their job.

00:16:43.290 --> 00:16:46.430
He's not micromanaging them.

00:16:46.430 --> 00:16:47.570
And so throughout that scene,

00:16:47.570 --> 00:16:48.920
there was a lot of great communication.

00:16:48.920 --> 00:16:50.498
And that's 9.5.

00:16:51.749 --> 00:16:53.349
9.5 is like a 10.

00:16:53.349 --> 00:16:56.430
I have to say 9.5 because, you know,

00:16:56.430 --> 00:16:58.510
Hollywood has to spice things up

00:16:58.510 --> 00:17:00.250
and dramatize things, so, I get that, so.

00:17:00.250 --> 00:17:03.265
But, you know, for me, I
would say that was a 9.5.

